#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""kibble: an arcade-style terminal dashboard for a droidkibble phone.

A pixel dog in the middle tells you whether the watchdog is being fed. Battery and memory
are on its left, network, DHCP and watchdog details on its right, and the bottom third is a
shell on the phone. It also switches headless mode on and off.

It only wraps ssh. Python 3 standard library only. Wants a terminal with 256 colors and about
100x30; on less it falls back to a small ASCII dog.

    kibble [ssh-host]            the dashboard. With no host it finds the phone by itself, and if it
                                 can't it offers to scan your network and helps you log in.
    kibble <ssh-host> --once     print one snapshot as text and exit (also handy in scripts)

    kibble find                  print the phone's current address (looks for it if it moved)
    kibble learn <ssh-host>      remember this phone (its address and SSH host key) for finding it later
    kibble connect [port]        an ssh ProxyCommand that follows the phone between networks
    kibble ssh-config [--install|--remove]   set that up for `phone-root` and `arch` in ~/.ssh/config

Keys in the dashboard:
    Enter            run the line in the current shell (Android root shell or the Arch chroot)
    Tab or Ctrl-T    switch between the two shells (each keeps its own directory)
    Left/Right/Home/End/Delete, Ctrl-A/E/W/K/U   edit the line
    Up / Down        command history          PgUp / PgDn   scroll the output
    While a command runs, what you type goes to it (answer a pacman [Y/n] prompt, for instance)
    Ctrl-L or `clear`  clear the output
    F5  or :headless   turn headless mode on/off right now (asks first)
    F6  or :boot       turn "go headless at boot" on/off (asks first)
    :connect           pick a different phone / log in again        :help   list these
    :q or Ctrl-D       quit                     Ctrl-C   ask a running command to stop, or quit when idle
Programs that need a terminal (top, vim, ...) don't work here; use ssh for those.
"""
